  "problem": "Find the numbers $c$ such that the average value of $f(x)=-9{x}^{2}+14x-2$ on the interval $[0,c]$ is equal to $0.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve for $c,$ we apply the average value formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{b-a}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c-0}{\\int }_{0}^{c}(-9{x}^{2}+14x-2)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 0 & ={\\frac{1}{c}(-3{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-2x)|}_{0}^{c} \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{1}{c}[(-3{c}^{3}+7{c}^{2}-2c)-0] \\\\ 0 & =-3{c}^{2}+7c-2 \\\\ 3{c}^{2}-7c+2 & =0 \\\\ (c-2)(3c-1) & =0 \\\\ c & =2,\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
